WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.436
Communication involves creating and interpreting meaning.

2
00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:07.879
A sender encodes a message, uses a channel, and a receiver decodes it.

3
00:00:08.079 --> 00:00:10.270
Feedback helps test what was understood.

4
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.936
Noise is anything that hinders the process, not only sound.

5
00:00:14.136 --> 00:00:18.891
Jargon, assumptions, interruptions, or an inaccessible format can interfere.

6
00:00:19.091 --> 00:00:27.195
At Cedar Crossing, the instruction make the captions accessible sounds clear until colleagues discover they mean different things by accessible.

7
00:00:27.395 --> 00:00:30.553
A shared example and a check-back can reveal the difference.

8
00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:39.189
Downward communication moves from supervisors to staff; upward communication carries information and concerns toward supervisors.

9
00:00:39.389 --> 00:00:44.310
Horizontal communication connects colleagues at the same organizational level.

10
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:51.299
Informal networks also carry useful information, but important decisions still need reliable confirmation.

11
00:00:51.499 --> 00:00:53.403
Choose a channel suited to the task.

12
00:00:53.603 --> 00:00:59.576
A written specification may support reference later; a conversation may help resolve ambiguity.

13
00:00:59.776 --> 00:01:03.580
Listen, ask for clarification, and confirm the agreed meaning.

14
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.930
Sending another copy of the same unclear message may simply repeat the original problem.
